{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition","id":985336,"title":"What's new in 2nd Edition?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-10-11T03:07:56-07:00","id":985336,"title":"What's new in 2nd Edition?","content":"It's been a long time that I played VtR, that was still during the time of nWoD and VtR 1st editions. I want to give it a try again.<br /> <br /> Now, both of them have a second edition, Chronicles of Darkness and VtR 2e. <br /> <br /> What are the major changes that I should look out for?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2337-quatar","name":"Quatar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"269"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-11T05:47:10-07:00","id":985365,"title":"","content":"Disciplines have been re-written extensively.<br /> <br /> Character creation no longer requires or involves the World of Darkness/Chronicles of Darkness core book. Vampire 2e does not require the Chronicles of Darkness corebook, but it helps.<br /> <br /> Instead of a virtue and a vice you pick a mask (how you are around humans) and a dirge (how you are around kindred), and those govern how you regain willpower.<br /> <br /> Clans no longer have clan weaknesses. Instead they have banes, which are definitely not the same - Mekhet don't just take extra damage from sunlight, for example. They have to take an extra bane when they lose humanity, and their Humanity is treated as one dot lower for all banes. They're roughly described in the clan descriptions, but are detailed further elsewhere.<br /> <br /> Humanity no longer deals with morality. Instead, actions and experiences that set you apart from humanity can cause a breaking point, which might cause humanity loss.<br /> <br /> The fog of ages is no longer a definite part of torpor. Kindred with 6+ dots in traits no longer lose those traits when blood potency drops below the level required to have them.<br /> <br /> Experience is a very different system: Everything has a flat cost in experiences. You earn experiences by earning &quot;beats.&quot; I think five beats equal an experience, and you can earn beats in a variety of ways.<br /> <br /> Bloodlines are constituted a bit differently in 2e. For examples, check out threads started by forum members or the official vampire resources sticky.<br /> <br /> This list is not exhaustive, just what comes to mind immediately.<br /> <br /> Read the book carefully and try not to carry assumptions from first edition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2367,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","user":"Resplendent Fire","date":". 10-11-2016, 09:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-11T06:05:42-07:00","id":985370,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Resplendent Fire</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=985365#post985365\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Instead they have banes, which are definitely not the same - Mekhet don't take extra damage from sunlight, for example..</div> </div> </div> </div>Mehket do suffer from extra sunlight damage as part of their bane.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-11T06:07:12-07:00","id":985371,"title":"","content":"The first assumption I made was &quot;I need the CoD book to understand VtR, as it was in 1st&quot;. So guess CoD is primarily the Mortal book, while VtR2 has all I need for a vampire game?<br /> <br /> And just noticed they still use the same, terrible font for headlines in VtR... it's really a pain to read if english isn't your first language. (and maybe even for you guys, I don't know)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2337-quatar","name":"Quatar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"269"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-11T06:13:21-07:00","id":985372,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=985370#post985370\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mehket do suffer from extra sunlight damage as part of their bane.</div> </div> </div> </div>Their humanity is considered to be one lower for the purpose of all Humanity-based banes, which does not <i>specifically</i> mean more damage in sunlight, although it usually does. It applies to more than just sunlight. You're right that I stated it incorrectly, though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Quatar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=985371#post985371\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The first assumption I made was &quot;I need the CoD book to understand VtR, as it was in 1st&quot;. So guess CoD is primarily the Mortal book, while VtR2 has all I need for a vampire game?<br /> <br /> And just noticed they still use the same, terrible font for headlines in VtR... it's really a pain to read if english isn't your first language. (and maybe even for you guys, I don't know)</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not a fan of cursive ever, so I kinda feel your pain.<br /> <br /> And yeah, CofD is for mortals, for the God-Machine Chronicle, and for more general rules. You don't <i>need</i> it, but having it can't hurt. VtR2 has everything you need for a Vampire game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2367,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","user":"Resplendent Fire","date":". 10-11-2016, 09:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-11T06:22:25-07:00","id":985377,"title":"","content":"Ok, thanks. I'll skip CoD for now then, and read VtR. I might read it later again, but from the sound of it, it has mostly stuff for the ST in there, and I'm more aiming at being a player for now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2337-quatar","name":"Quatar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"269"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-11T06:31:08-07:00","id":985380,"title":"","content":"It does have player stuff, such as merits and flaws.<br /> <br /> To be fair I haven't compared the lists to see if the merits and flaws are duplicated in Vampire.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2367,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","user":"Resplendent Fire","date":". 10-11-2016, 09:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-11T06:42:19-07:00","id":985383,"title":"","content":"I'll make sure to check both books then for that. But I meant in terms of actual game rules etc, there's nothing in CoD that I need that isn't repeated in VtR, correct?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2337-quatar","name":"Quatar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"269"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-11T06:43:52-07:00","id":985385,"title":"","content":"That is correct.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-11T07:24:00-07:00","id":985401,"title":"","content":"Depends. The vampire book doesn't have all the fighting styles in the Vampire book (as well as a the psi power merits), and a few minor rules were... forgotten I guess ? Like the foot chase rules : Referenced in Vampire Requiem 2e, but not actually included. The fighting styles are pretty cool, I think, so that's something. Plus, the rules for ephemeral beings is in CoD if you ever need it and I kinda like the whole god machine bit. At any rate, it can be inspiring.<br /> <br /> For Vampire, the whole system is, I think, a more streamlined version of the first edition, incorporating a few good ideas from supplements. The combat feels like it has more potential tactical depths than before, which is very welcome. The disciplines are more intelligently done than before too, like Resilience which is finally good for something and Celerity which is great at long last (not the uber broken action multiplier of Masquerade and not the, really useless version of Requiem 1ed)<br /> <br /> Then, there are other things I'm parted on. I don't like the Carhtians and Invictus' new supernatural powers from nowhere There's a social maneuvering system that feels really unnecessary to me, the experience system I find overly complicated and requires everyone to keep track of a bunch of non-plot related things, although I can appreciate the basic idea. The touchstones are hit and miss I guess (some people reported it working great, my players how no interest in it). Finally there's the condition system. Good idea, but there are so much I find the system a bit confusing to keep a track of. As a GM, I have enough work trying to make everything fit, roleplaying the NPCs and maintaining the right atmosphere to be worrying if Alice still got her &quot;Competitive&quot; condition of the penalties she must take and knowing when it's resolved or disappears. They're essential to play because a few disciplines revolve around them, but I usually forget about the rest. The physical cards feel almost mandatory if you want to really play with the conditions without having to open the book every five minutes because a new condition appears or you forgot exactly what the effect was. It's a pretty demanding system in terms of attention.<br /> <br /> All in all, I really think it feels much more complete than 1ed. It's just a matter of adjusting a few things if you don't like them, and it's doable with a few tweaks, generally.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7104-s-brechen","name":"S. Brechen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"171"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-11T10:48:18-07:00","id":985508,"title":"","content":"Actually the Oaths and Laws were in 1E. I can't remember which book started the Laws, but the Oaths first turned up in the Invictus covenant book.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5439-gullinbursti","name":"Gullinbursti","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5439_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"291"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-11T11:03:18-07:00","id":985515,"title":"","content":"Check out <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/114078/World-of-Darkness-GodMachine-Rules-Update?\" target=\"_blank\">The God-Machine Chronicle Rules Update</a>. It's free, and it explains the 2nd Edition core rules in terms of how they're different from the 1st Edition core rules, so you can quickly grasp the basic changes without having to read through everything that still works the same way. The changes to vampire-specific rules are a bit more extensive, so for Requiem you'll probably want to read everything and discard assumptions from 1e. But the GMC update chapter is a nice, free way to sample the 2e basic rules and decide if they're right for you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-11T11:10:19-07:00","id":985521,"title":"","content":"Thanks, I'll read that","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2337-quatar","name":"Quatar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"269"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-11T11:40:05-07:00","id":985550,"title":"","content":"In more specific terms on what's <i>not</i> in the Vampire 2e core:<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>Free Rules Update</b></span><br /> The GMC Rules Update Charlaquin linked contains the rules for <b>Integrity,</b> the mortal trait which occupies the role of 1e's Morality, and is replaced by Humanity for vampires. Integrity is a fairly simple system and a Vampire game that doesn't have a strong focus on specific ghoul or mortal characters can probably run smoothly without it, but it is referenced a couple times. For instance, a couple of the banes which can be accumulated through detachment from Humanity respond to mortals with high Integrity.<br /> The Rules Update has an extended treatment of <b>Virtue and Vice,</b> the Anchors that mortals use instead of 2e vampires' Mask and Dirge. You don't really need this, both because an understanding of 1e Virtue and Vice slots right in easily and because the 2e Vampire book does cover them, just in less detail, but you might be interested in it if you like the sound of 2e's decision to make them freeform instead of selected from a 7x7 list. (If you like the 7x7 list better, you lose nothing by running it that way.)<br /> Many of the <b>universal Merits</b> which can be taken by both vampires and mortals are omitted in the Vampire core for space constraints. The Rules Update also contains <b>Supernatural Merits</b> which are unavailable to major supernatural templates like vampires, but may be fitting for strange NPCs in roles as antagonists or supporting cast.<br /> The Rules Update contains the <b>Tilts system,</b> which is like a set of combat-specific status and environmental effects, like torrential downpours, being temporarily blinded, or being knocked to the ground. A combat-heavy game might find good use for these.<br /> The Rules Update covers what happens when a mortal <b>loses his soul.</b> This is not generally important for a Vampire game.<br /> The Rules Update contains the rules for <b>ephemeral entities,</b> covering <b>ghosts, spirits, and angels.</b> These aren't essential for a Vampire game, but might be interesting as outside-context problems and antagonists. The Vampire core's section on the strix mentions that they resemble the rules for ephemeral entities, but in terms of actually running the strix mechanically, the Vampire core material is already complete and requires no cross-referencing.<br /> The Rules Update has some extra <b>supernatural equipment</b> and special cursed <b>bygone</b> objects. Again, not essential, maybe interesting to use if you want.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>Chronicles of Darkness Rulebook</b></span><br /> The CofD rulebook contains <b>everything listed above in the Rules Update except for</b> supernatural equipment, bygones, and the full writeup for soul loss (it contains a truncated version). So if you find the above material handy and want a hard copy to reference at the table, the CofD rulebook has that stuff.<br /> It contains <b>optional subsystems</b> for <b>vehicles, foot chases, investigation and clues, building equipment</b> (including abstract equipment like elaborate heist plans), and <b>Horrors,</b> which are miscellaneous, often unique monsters or supernatural forces, ranging from precognitive mothmen to doppelgangers climbing out of mirrors to swarms of zombies.<br /> It has several new <b>Merits</b> that aren't in the Rules Update, about half of which plug into the optional subsystems.<br /> And it has an extended setting riff on the God-Machine with various modular <b>adventure hooks and NPC writeups</b> for humans, angels, and oil rigs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T04:24:55-07:00","id":986041,"title":"","content":"BTW the weakness Mekhet lost is that they no longer take extra damage from <i>fire</i>.<br /> <br /> That said, all of the clan banes were changed at least slightly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T05:10:13-07:00","id":986057,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=986041#post986041\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">BTW the weakness Mekhet lost is that they no longer take extra damage from <i>fire</i>.<br /> <br /> That said, all of the clan banes were changed at least slightly.</div> </div> </div> </div>And they take extra damage from <i>potentially</i> so many other things, depending on chosen banes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T08:14:48-07:00","id":986145,"title":"","content":"They've also made some alterations to how damage is dealt in combat. It seems a bit more vicious. All weapons deal lethal damage now (though I think vampires downgrade lethal damage, so they'll have an even easier time slaughtering mortals), and weapon damage adds a flat bonus to your success rather than adding dice to your roll.<br /> <br /> The tilts and conditions system is confusing but interesting. I haven't had the courage to introduce it to my game yet, but I'll have to soon. It's one of the best ways for characters to gain experience.<br /> <br /> That said, the new beat/experience system requires more bookkeeping but is actually pretty great because it rewards players for encouraging failures on the part of their characters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T18:23:14-07:00","id":986504,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>dxanders</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=986145#post986145\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That said, the new beat/experience system requires more bookkeeping but is actually pretty great because it rewards players for encouraging failures on the part of their characters.</div> </div> </div> </div>It only seems like more bookkeeping because of the names. If it bothers you, just call Beats experience points and multiply the exp cost of everything by 5.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T18:33:31-07:00","id":986506,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=986504#post986504\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It only seems like more bookkeeping because of the names. If it bothers you, just call Beats experience points and multiply the exp cost of everything by 5.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Sorry, misquote on my end. It's the condition/tilt rules that intimidate me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T18:40:24-07:00","id":986509,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>dxanders</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=986506#post986506\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sorry, misquote on my end. It's the condition/tilt rules that intimidate me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Gotcha. Personally, I don't get why. 1e had just as many floating bonuses and penalties and triggers for other effects and what have you, they just weren't packaged in convenient little boxes called Conditions. I've been using note cards to keep track of things like the effects of being under the influence of Magesty 3 (or whatever) for years, it's just that now Onyx Path makes them for me and sells them on laminated cards, and players have more reason to be proactive in tracking them because they're one of the main sources of experience.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T18:46:30-07:00","id":986511,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=986509#post986509\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Gotcha. Personally, I don't get why. 1e had just as many floating bonuses and penalties and triggers for other effects and what have you, they just weren't packaged in convenient little boxes called Conditions. I've been using note cards to keep track of things like the effects of being under the influence of Magesty 3 (or whatever) for years, it's just that now Onyx Path makes them for me and sells them on laminated cards, and players have more reason to be proactive in tracking them because they're one of the main sources of experience.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Interesting! In all honesty, it's probably more that this is the first time I've GMed in years. I'd probably have the same trouble with floating effects in 1E. It's just that I can ignore conditions for the first couple of sessions in 2E, under pretext of easing my players into the systems. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-13T02:04:13-07:00","id":986639,"title":"","content":"Well, one of the problems with the convenient little condition boxes is that they're all at the end of the book, so when I look at disciplines, I have to be ready to jump to that section and back multiple times. I've never really felt the need for reminder papers in 1E. Not sure why, there were lots of things to take into account, but it never felt like much, honestly. Maybe it's the way my memory works, I don't know.<br /> <br /> I understand the reasoning that it pushes players to get in trouble so they'll get more experience. Honestly, it's smart. That said, I find it a little artificial as means to advance plot. Players should be getting in trouble because their characters want to. It's their job to come up with characters who will be at least a little proactive and, more importantly, it's the GM's job to shape the story so that every character can find a decent motivation not to just pack up and leave (it also means setting up an engaging enough atmosphere and making the whole thing intriguing enough). It's difficult to do that consistently (and vampire never really encouraged the camaraderie that can make it easier in some other games), but every GM should aim for that, and every player should contribute a little to that goal.<br /> <br /> I feel about the new experience system and the way it ties in with critical failures, tilts and conditions the same way I feel about social maneuvering. Functionally, it's smart, but I don't want my players to fail because they want more XP, I want them to fail because they made a stupid decision, or because they bit off more than they could chew or because they were really unlucky (if they do want more XP, they usually just tell me and we talk about it). The same way I don't want my players to think &quot;ok, so I gotta open this guy's doors so I'll try forcing one and...&quot; but &quot;ok, I get the impression this guy thinks that way, so I should try to pretend like I agree with him for now, maybe he'll like me and spit out some information&quot;. Ok, the two social situations are pretty much the same, but one feels to me like some &quot;roleplaying for dummies&quot; thing. It would be a good system in a video game, but I feel an pen and paper RPG should be more organic. Physical combat needs a lot of abstraction, because it's not something anybody can replicate. Roleplaying gives us this opportunity to be very nuanced. The doors system should be the bare bones form of roleplaying, not something to follow all the time. Sometimes, &quot;doors&quot; don't matter, sometimes, the player will start talking and say one particular word or phrase, and that phrase alone will unexpectedly get the NPC's attention the way maybe a exceptional success would have. Maybe an elder doesn't like it when people talk to him informally, maybe the sheriff will laugh at the ventrue PC's face when they start adressing him as &quot;sir&quot; because he doesn't bother with formalities. It's better to let players find these things out via roleplay than letting them know they adopted the wrong tone after a failed roll.<br /> <br /> Again, I think I see the point of those systems, and they do make sense. Guess they're just not for me. After all, I mostly discovered RPGs with Feng Shui, which had a super light system. Besides, the social maneuvering thingie can be omitted without losing anything basically except the fast talk debating style. Some of the other systems are a little bit more engrained into the mechanics, which demands a bit more work to ignore them, but so far, it worked.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7104-s-brechen","name":"S. Brechen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"171"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-14T08:40:42-07:00","id":987270,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>S. Brechen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=986639#post986639\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, one of the problems with the convenient little condition boxes is that they're all at the end of the book, so when I look at disciplines, I have to be ready to jump to that section and back multiple times.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, that's not a problem for me because I use PDFs and can easily jump to the bookmark for the appendix in question. If you prefer analogue, you can always use physical bookmarks. Sticky notes work great for this. And, of course, there's always the POD laminate Condition and Tilt cards. Or handmade notecards if those are outside your budget.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>S. Brechen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=986639#post986639\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've never really felt the need for reminder papers in 1E. Not sure why, there were lots of things to take into account, but it never felt like much, honestly. Maybe it's the way my memory works, I don't know.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't blame you, memory is weird like that. I'm the opposite way, I have a much easier time remembering the effects of Conditions and Tilts than I ever did the various floating bonuses and penalties that were all hidden away in individual abilities in 1e. Something about having them all in the same place for reference makes it easier for me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>S. Brechen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=986639#post986639\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I understand the reasoning that it pushes players to get in trouble so they'll get more experience. Honestly, it's smart. That said, I find it a little artificial as means to advance plot. Players should be getting in trouble because their characters want to. It's their job to come up with characters who will be at least a little proactive and, more importantly, it's the GM's job to shape the story so that every character can find a decent motivation not to just pack up and leave (it also means setting up an engaging enough atmosphere and making the whole thing intriguing enough). It's difficult to do that consistently (and vampire never really encouraged the camaraderie that can make it easier in some other games), but every GM should aim for that, and every player should contribute a little to that goal.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, absolutely. That's part of the buy-in for the game. I don't see Conditions as a substitute for player buy-in. Rather, I see them as a way to leave the characters' agency in the player's hands. 1e was chock full of powers that forced characters to take certain actions, especially in Vampire. In 2e, the majority of those forced actions have been replaced by Conditions that still allow the player agency in deciding if their character takes the action or not, but rewards them for taking it. Dominate being the notable exception, but even that gives the player a Beat as a consolation for having their agency temporarily suspended.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>S. Brechen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=986639#post986639\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I feel about the new experience system and the way it ties in with critical failures, tilts and conditions the same way I feel about social maneuvering. Functionally, it's smart, but I don't want my players to fail because they want more XP, I want them to fail because they made a stupid decision, or because they bit off more than they could chew or because they were really unlucky (if they do want more XP, they usually just tell me and we talk about it). The same way I don't want my players to think &amp;quot;ok, so I gotta open this guy's doors so I'll try forcing one and...&amp;quot; but &amp;quot;ok, I get the impression this guy thinks that way, so I should try to pretend like I agree with him for now, maybe he'll like me and spit out some information&amp;quot;. Ok, the two social situations are pretty much the same, but one feels to me like some &amp;quot;roleplaying for dummies&amp;quot; thing. It would be a good system in a video game, but I feel an pen and paper RPG should be more organic.</div> </div> </div> </div>Interesting. That's... Not how I run Social Maneuvering. I use Doors and Impressions as a Storyteller tool, to help <i>me</i> track the progress PCs are making in socially manipulating NPCs. I don't present it in terms of &quot;you have opened one Door, leaving the Seneshal with three more Doors and your impression is Good, so you will have to wait until tomorrow to try to open the next one or try to Force his Doors now.&quot; I describe the scenario in in-character terms, and the player describes their actions in in-character terms. The systems are tools to make it easier for the Storyteller to adjudicate those actions. And honestly? Players who are going to think in terms of mechanics instead of narrative are going to do so no matter what system you use. It's not my job to police how my players make decisions for their characters, my job is to describe the scenario, adjudicate actions, and describe the results.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>S. Brechen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=986639#post986639\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Physical combat needs a lot of abstraction, because it's not something anybody can replicate. Roleplaying gives us this opportunity to be very nuanced. The doors system should be the bare bones form of roleplaying, not something to follow all the time.</div> </div> </div> </div>You know Social Maneuvering isn't an all or nothing System, right? You can use it some times and use standard instant and/or extended Social actions other times. I personally use it for all social interactions, but I've come to accept that I'm the weird one for doing that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>S. Brechen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=986639#post986639\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sometimes, &amp;quot;doors&amp;quot; don't matter, sometimes, the player will start talking and say one particular word or phrase, and that phrase alone will unexpectedly get the NPC's attention the way maybe a exceptional success would have. Maybe an elder doesn't like it when people talk to him informally, maybe the sheriff will laugh at the ventrue PC's face when they start adressing him as &amp;quot;sir&amp;quot; because he doesn't bother with formalities.</div> </div> </div> </div>This comes down to adjudication 101. If the reaults of an action are not uncertain, don't use dice to determine the results. If a particular word or phrase is sure to get an NPC's attention, don't even bother rolling, all that does is introduce a chance of failure where none should exist. Likewise, rolling dice when addressing the Sheriff who doesn't put stock in titles as &quot;sir&quot; to see how he reacts to it introduces a chance of success where none should exist. The Social Maneuvering system shouldn't come into play in these situations because you don't need any system to figure out the results of these actions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>S. Brechen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=986639#post986639\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's better to let players find these things out via roleplay than letting them know they adopted the wrong tone after a failed roll.</div> </div> </div> </div>In my opinion, these things should be conveyed through in-character information regardless of if dice are involved or not. The roll is just used as a neutral arbiter to decide the results of actions with uncertain outcomes. But the actions and the outcomes should still be communicated through roleplay.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>S. Brechen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/985336-what-s-new-in-2nd-edition?p=986639#post986639\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Again, I think I see the point of those systems, and they do make sense. Guess they're just not for me. After all, I mostly discovered RPGs with Feng Shui, which had a super light system. Besides, the social maneuvering thingie can be omitted without losing anything basically except the fast talk debating style. Some of the other systems are a little bit more engrained into the mechanics, which demands a bit more work to ignore them, but so far, it worked.</div> </div> </div> </div>For sure. You're not alone in feeling that way, and if they're not for you, they're not for you. It's all good. I mostly just like to explain my approach to them to hopefully show folks a perspective on them they might not have considered. If you still don't care for them,by all means do what you've got to do to make the game work for you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-14T14:23:58-07:00","id":987442,"title":"","content":"Hey, thanks for the response, it's cool. I guess I see your point. I still stand by my opinions mostly, but that's just what I found to be working for me and my players. The social maneuvering thing, well, I've done without an elaborate system all my gaming life so far, so seeing something like that pop out all of a sudden really made me scratch my head as something I didn't need. Eh, it's ok anyway.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I still think it's a great system in general and agree with most 2ed changes. The only thing I wish is that it was more easy to use the condition system while taking out the XP earning system. I kinda like the other effects resolving conditions can have, though. There's a ventrue player at my table and at some point, as they knew they were about to face a powerful ventrue, they thought &quot;hey, let's have this guy dominate us, then slap us so we can resist dominate better later on&quot;. They then proceeded to try letting themselves be dominated, then slapped. Then, on NPC that was accompanying them would continually resist until I roleplayed him faking getting dominated so they would just move on, just letting a barely perceptible sigh just before the slap.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7104-s-brechen","name":"S. Brechen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"171"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}